    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d811">

                <pb facs="1106" n="986" sameAs="frus:frus1922v01/pg986"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">211.18/27</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Minister in Costa Rica</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Davis</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1922-08-22"><hi rendition="#italic">August 22,
                        1922</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 91</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: The Department has received your despatch
                    No. 51 of June 15, 1922, respecting the ratification of the Extradition
                    Convention by the Congress of Costa Rica.</p>
                <p>You are instructed to advise the Foreign Office that this Government cannot agree
                    to the modification as indicated in Article 2 of the <pb facs="1107" n="987"
                        xml:id="pg987"/> Costa Rican decree of ratification. You may indicate,
                    however, the willingness of this Government to effect an exchange of notes
                    similar to that which followed the signing of the Extradition Convention with
                    Portugal in 1908 as set forth at pages 1475 and 1476 in the Compilation entitled
                        “<hi rendition="#italic">Treaties, Conventions, International Acts,
                        Protocols and Agreements Between the United States and Other
                        Powers”.</hi><anchor n="13" xml:id="d811-f1"/></p>
                <p>In advising and consenting to the ratification of the Extradition Convention with
                    Portugal, the Senate of the United States expressed its understanding that the
                    agreement just before mentioned would, in effect, form part of the treaty.</p>
                <p>In conclusion you may express the hope that the Government of Costa Rica will
                    acquiesce in the plan proposed for an exchange of notes embodying substantially
                    the modification now expressed in Article 2 of the Costa Rican decree of
                    ratification.</p>
                <p>I am [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Charles E. Hughes</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
